# All 1016 stars with apparent visual magnitude V <= +4.60, excluding the Sun.
#
# Columns: (formal name, other names or None, RA [deg], Dec [deg], spectral type,
#   V mag, magnitude type ('V'), pm RA * cos(Dec) [mas/yr], pm Dec [mas/yr],
#   parallax [mas])
#
# Positions: ICRS / J2000.0 epoch and equinox, from the HYG v4.1 database, which
#   takes them from the Hipparcos catalog (typical accuracy ~1 mas, well under 1").
#   Given here to 1e-6 deg (0.0036").
# Proper motions and parallaxes: Hipparcos, the new reduction (van Leeuwen 2007, CDS
#   I/311), matched by HIP number. The proper motion in RA is mu_alpha* = d(RA)/dt *
#   cos(Dec), as catalogs conventionally give it. The parallax of the distant supergiant
#   HR 4352 is published as -0.02 +/- 0.30 mas; it is kept as published, and a star
#   whose parallax is not positive is treated as having none.
# Spectral types: Yale Bright Star Catalog, 5th ed. (fallback: Hipparcos via HYG).
# Magnitudes: HYG v4.1 (Hipparcos-derived Johnson V); combined light for unresolved
#   multiples. Many bright stars are variable, so treat the ranking as approximate.
# Formal names: Bayer designation, else Flamsteed, else GCVS variable-star
#   designation, else HR (Bright Star) number. R Lyrae (13 Lyrae) is named
#   by its GCVS designation.
# Other names: a string or a list of strings, the IAU-approved proper name as listed
#   in HYG v4.1 first, followed by the HR number of a star whose formal name is a
#   variable-star designation.
# Only stars with a Hipparcos entry are included, so each resolved component
#   (e.g. Alpha1/Alpha2 Centauri) appears once. The list is complete to V = +4.60 as
#   given by HYG (2 decimals), so there is no partial tie at the cutoff.
#
# Sources: HYG database (CC BY-SA 4.0), github.com/astronexus/HYG-Database;
#   Hipparcos new reduction (CDS I/311);
#   Yale BSC5 via github.com/brettonw/YaleBrightStarCatalog.

